    {
      "note": "אֵי־זֶה is a maqqef-bound fixed interrogative expression that the ASV renders by the single English word 'where', so the two morphemes share one unit headed on the lexeme-bearing אֵי (rule 30) rather than leaving the enclitic זֶה unrepresented beside a rendered אֵי. The interrogative is the verbless clause's predicate, so the construction-supplied copula 'is' joins the unit (rule 20), which rule 30 allows for a fused expression. The competing reading aligns אֵי alone to 'where is' and marks זֶה unrepresented; it fails rule 30's one-English-word condition, which is met here because no morpheme owns a part of 'where'. This is not the לָמָּה־זֶּה of JER.6.20, where the ASV spends 'To' on לְ and 'what' on מָה and the condition is defeated.",
      "source": [
